; Regression: two instantiations of one generic free function must not share a
; SYMBOL (2026-09-04). Sibling of generic_free_function.scaly.
;
; It exercises the STDLIB's `allocate_slice[T]` rather than a locally declared
; twin, because that is where the collision was measured: a function declared
; in the compiled ROOT is re-planned per call and did not reproduce it.
;
; Itanium encodes the PARAMETER types, which distinguishes two instantiations
; whenever T appears in the signature. It distinguishes NOTHING when T appears
; only in the RETURN type: `allocate_slice[T](host, n) returns Slice[T]` mangled
; identically for every T, the first definition won under linkonce_odr, and
; every other call landed in it — a `[u64]` call reaching a `[u8]` body and
; allocating 4 bytes where it asked for 32.
;
; ★★★THE NARROW INSTANTIATION MUST COME FIRST, AND IT MUST BE THE FIRST IN THE
; WHOLE FILE. Under the defect the winner is whichever is instantiated first;
; with the wide one first every later call merely OVER-allocates and everything
; reads back correctly. That is why this lives in its own file rather than in
; the sibling, which instantiates [u64] near the top for its own purposes.
;
; ★The check is the ALLOCATION SIZE, not a value. The defect answers plausible
; values — the bump allocator has room behind the short buffer, so the writes
; land, read back correctly, and overrun whatever follows. Two consecutive
; allocations from one page are adjacent, so the distance between their buffers
; IS the size the first one took: 4 under the defect, at least 32 when the
; instantiation is its own.
;
; Expected: PASS

use scaly.memory.Page

let rp Page.allocate_root_page()
let host rp as ref[Page]

var ok true

; the NARROW instantiation first — it is the one that would win
var narrow allocate_slice[u8](host, 4 as size_t)
narrow.put(0 as size_t, 3 as u8)

var w1 allocate_slice[u64](host, 4 as size_t)
var w2 allocate_slice[u64](host, 4 as size_t)
let gap (w2.data as size_t) - (w1.data as size_t)
if gap < (32 as size_t)
    ok := false

w1.put(3 as size_t, 4444 as u64)
if (w1[3 as size_t] as int) <> 4444
    ok := false
if (narrow[0 as size_t] as int) <> 3
    ok := false

if ok
    print "PASS"
if ok = false
    print "FAIL"
